{
 "id": "2023-shen-design",
 "name": "Design Intensity in Urban Forests: Preference and Complexity",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the viewing and rating task in Shen, Wang, Liu, Luo, Liu & Lan (2023), \"Landscape Design Intensity and Its Associated Complexity of Forest Landscapes in Relation to Preference and Eye Movements\", Forests 14(4):761, doi:10.3390/f14040761. The 24 photographs are the authors' own (some edited by photomontage), taken in parks in Fuzhou, China, and printed in Figure 1 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0). They are used with credit for research, and the template is taken down if the authors object.\n\nWhat participants do: for each of three settings (lawn, path, waterscape) the page randomly picks photo group A or B, giving 12 photos with slight, low, medium and high landscape design intensity. Part 1: each photo is shown for 10 s after a centre dot, in random order, with no task (free viewing). Part 2: each photo is shown again and rated for preference and perceived complexity on 7-point scales. The page ends with gender and age.\n\nImages: <setting><group>_<level>, e.g. pathA_3medium. Design intensity was judged by landscape architects from the amount of designed elements (sculptures, lamps, benches, pavilions, flower beds and so on).\n\nOriginal study: 76 people aged 20 to 58 (37 men, 39 women), lab session with eye tracking in September 2020. Analysis: Kruskal-Wallis tests of preference and complexity across design intensities within each setting; regression of preference on subjective complexity and fractal dimension; marginal effects of design intensity. Subjective complexity rose with design intensity and predicted preference; fractal dimension did not.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) No eye tracking; online on any screen instead of a lab monitor at 50 cm with a chin rest and calibration before each image. (2) The duration of the centre dot is not reported; 2 s is used. (3) The paper says participants were randomly assigned one group per setting but not how; here A or B is drawn independently for each setting. (4) The scale anchors are not reported; \"not at all / very much\" (preference) and \"very simple / very complex\" (complexity) were added, and photos are rated one at a time in a new random order. (5) The pictures come from the printed figure (about 480 × 355 pixels each).",
